% csplib_002_template_design: a Picat model from DCP Rosetta.
%
% This problem arises from a colour printing firm which produces a variety of products from thin board,
% including cartons for human and animal food and magazine inserts. Food products, for example, are often
% marketed as a basic brand with several variations (typically flavours). Packaging for such variations usually
% has the same overall design, in particular the same size and shape, but differs in a small proportion of the
% text displayed and/or in colour. For example, two variations of a cat food carton may differ only in that on
% one is printed 'Chicken Flavour' on a blue background whereas the other has 'Rabbit Flavour' printed on a
% green background. A typical order is for a variety of quantities of several design variations. Because each
% variation is identical in dimension, we know in advance exactly how many items can be printed on each mother
% sheet of board, whose dimensions are largely determined by the dimensions of the printing machinery. Each
% mother sheet is printed from a template, consisting of a thin aluminium sheet on which the design for several
% of the variations is etched. The problem is to decide, for a given number of templates with a given number of
% slots each, which variations, and how many copies of each, to include on each template, and how many sheets to
% make from each template. Every slot of a template is filled. Each template yields between 1 sheet and as many
% sheets as the largest demand. The sheets printed must meet at least the demand of every variation, and the
% objective is to minimize the total number of printed sheets.
%
% Save this file as model.pi. Download `csplib_002_template_design.json` and `dcp_convert_picat.py` from the
% problem's page:
%   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_002_template_design.html
% Convert the instances once, then run the first one (Picat 3.9, https://picat-lang.org):
%   python dcp_convert_picat.py csplib_002_template_design.json
%   picat model.pi csplib_002_template_design_1.dat
%
% Data.get(n_templates): the number of templates; Data.get(n_slots): the number of slots of each template;
% Data.get(n_var): the number of variations; Data.get(demand): the demand for each variation (lists indexed
% from 1).
import cp.

model(Data, Vars, Outputs, Options) =>
    NumTemplates = Data.get(n_templates),
    NumSlots = Data.get(n_slots),
    NumVars = Data.get(n_var),
    Demand = Data.get(demand),
    MaxDemand = max(Demand),
    % Layout[T,V] = the number of copies of variation V on template T
    Layout = new_array(NumTemplates, NumVars),
    Layout :: 0..NumSlots,
    % every slot of a template is filled
    foreach (T in 1..NumTemplates)
        sum([Layout[T,V] : V in 1..NumVars]) #= NumSlots
    end,
    % Production[T] = the number of sheets printed from template T, between 1 and the largest demand
    Production = new_list(NumTemplates),
    Production :: 1..MaxDemand,
    % the sheets printed meet at least the demand of every variation
    foreach (V in 1..NumVars)
        sum([Production[T] * Layout[T,V] : T in 1..NumTemplates]) #>= Demand[V]
    end,
    % the total number of printed sheets
    TotalSheets #= sum(Production),
    Vars = [Production, Layout],
    Outputs = [production = Production, layout = Layout],
    % minimize the total number of printed sheets
    Options = [$min(TotalSheets)].

main([File]) =>
    Fd = open(File),
    Term = read_term(Fd),
    Term = $json_object(Keys, Values),
    close(Fd),
    Data = new_map([K = V : {K, V} in zip(Keys, Values)]),
    model(Data, Vars, Outputs, Options),
    solve(Options, Vars),
    solve(vars(Outputs)),
    foreach(Name = Value in Outputs)
        printf("%w = %w\n", Name, Value)
    end.
